    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d128">

                <pb facs="0242" n="126" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg126"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 793.94/308.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-23T19:00:00+08:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 23, 1915—7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>At the conference of April 19 no progress was made as the Japanese withheld
                    agreement on Manchuria and the Chinese refused to commit themselves on Group V
                    or make further concessions respecting the Hanyehping Company.</p>
                <p>As the negotiations had thus come to a deadlock it was necessary for the Japanese
                    Government to decide whether to accept the liberal concessions made by China or
                    to attempt to create a new basis of action. As both of the conferences were
                    prorogued at the request of Japan it is evident that an important decision is
                    impending based upon the situation here and taking into account the attitude of
                    the Powers.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
